+Four fields and no payload. `attempts` is how many tries it has spent, and the sentence is what its
+last attempt died with: between them they are what you need to decide, and the payload is
+deliberately not here — a queue that prints a customer's data into a scrollback, a ticket and a
+pastebin has turned one incident into two. `jobqc inspect <ID> --json` has the payload, for the
+case where you actually have to read it.
+
+The reason on the line is quoted, because a handler's sentence can contain a newline and one line
+per job is worth keeping: use `--json` when a script is reading.
 
 ## The two things you can do
 
 ```sh
-jobqc dlq replay <ID> --by <NAME> --reason <TEXT>
-jobqc dlq discard <ID> --by <NAME> --reason <TEXT>
+jobqc dlq replay 4f1c2f0a-… --by ada --reason "the email server is back"
+jobqc dlq discard 9a02b1c7-… --by ada --reason "the customer withdrew the request"
 ```
 
-<!-- TODO: what each act does to the row, and what each one costs. A replay is the reversible
-half: which fields of the job survive it, which one moves, and when the next worker takes it. A
-discard is the other half: what it removes, why the reason is required for exactly one of the two
-commands, and what the name is for (it is a record, not a credential — say where "may I" is
-answered). -->
+**A replay gives the job one more attempt.** The count it has spent is kept, the ceiling moves to one
+past it, the reason the last life died stays on the row, and the job becomes due straight away — so
+the next worker that asks will take it. If it was wrong, the job is back in its queue with its
+payload and its counters, which is what makes this the safe half.
+
+**A discard throws the job away.** It is gone: the row, its payload, and its trail of what happened
+to it. Nothing can be reconstructed from what is left, which is why the command refuses to run
+without `--reason`, and why the tool needs a name from you for both of them:
+
+```sh
+jobqc dlq discard 9a02b1c7-… --by ada          # exit 2, and nothing moves
+```
+
+The name is not a credential — this store trusts the handle it is handed, and `jobq/3.13-queue-authz-attribution`
+is where "may I" is answered. It is a record: the tool writes down who acted and why, and an
+operator reading the record a week later is reading what somebody claimed, which is the difference
+between an act with a name on it and an act with nothing.
 
 ## What it remembers
 
+Every act writes one line:
+
 ```sh
-jobqc dlq audit
+jobqc dlq audit --limit 20
+```
+
+```text
+2030-01-01T02:14:03+00:00 discard 9a02b1c7-… signup ada the customer withdrew the request
+2030-01-01T02:11:47+00:00 replay 4f1c2f0a-… signup ada the email server is back
 ```
 
-<!-- TODO: the record of acts, as a shape: one line per act, newest first, the fields it carries,
-and what a refused act leaves behind. Say which line survives a job that is gone, and where the
-record lives that lets it. -->
+One line per *act*, newest first: a refused act leaves no line at all (nothing happened), a look
+leaves none (an inspect that writes is an inspect an auditor cannot use), and the line carries the
+queue it was about, because after a discard there is no row left to ask. The record outlives every
+job in it — it is a table of its own, with no foreign key — and that is the whole of its value: for
+a replay the row is still there and its own trail says what happened to it, but for a discard this
+line is the only account there will be.
 
 ## The codes a script branches on
 
 | code | what it means | what to do |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| `0` | TODO | TODO |
-| `2` | TODO | TODO |
-| `3` | TODO | TODO |
-| `4` | TODO | TODO |
-| `1` | TODO | TODO |
+| `0` | the command answered | read stdout |
+| `2` | the call was wrong | fix the command: an unknown subcommand, a missing `--by` or `--reason`, a reason that is only whitespace, a job that is not a dead letter |
+| `3` | the thing asked about is not there | the identity is wrong, or somebody else already acted on it |
+| `4` | the store refused | the queue would not take it; this is the retryable class |
+| `1` | this deployment is broken | the settings, a file, or a connection — this is not about the call |
 
